<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s1">
  <p class="st-k rv">01 · Challenge</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s1">Strategy in words, planning in numbers</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Starting a business means making many decisions before you know whether they will work.
    Strategy is usually discussed in words and financial planning in numbers, yet the two are
    tightly linked.
  </p>
  <div class="st-grid st-g4 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Question 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Pricing</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">How much should the product cost?</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Question 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Break-even</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">How many customers do you need to break even?</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Question 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Costs</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">What happens if rent goes up?</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Question 04</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Funding</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">How much funding will you need?</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Spreadsheet models are powerful, but they assume financial modelling skills many people don’t
    have. The challenge was not only to calculate the numbers but to help people understand what the
    numbers mean for their decisions.
  </p>
  <div class="st-callout rv">
    <p class="st-bh">
      How might we make business strategy and financial modelling easier to understand, explore and
      act on, without burying people in financial complexity?
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s2">
  <p class="st-k rv">02 · Who it’s for</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s2">Three people, very different expertise</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    The product had to be simple enough for a beginner without limiting an expert. That tension
    shaped most of the decisions below.
  </p>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">User 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">First-time entrepreneurs</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        Need a simple starting point, help turning an idea into a model, and a readable view of what
        might happen.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">User 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Accountants</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        Need a structured model where every assumption and output can be checked.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">User 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Business advisors</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        Need to explain financial consequences, test other scenarios and talk a plan through with a
        client.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">Five principles behind the design</h3>
  <ol class="st-list st-wide rv">
    <li>Make complexity approachable</li>
    <li>Connect inputs to outcomes</li>
    <li>Make AI understandable</li>
    <li>Support exploration</li>
    <li>Design for every level of expertise</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s3">
  <p class="st-k rv">03 · Defining the experience</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s3">Strategy made visible</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    One idea guided the design: don’t just show people the numbers, help them understand the
    decisions behind them.
  </p>
  <div class="st-stats st-wide rv">
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">8</p>
      <p>assumption categories, from Setup to Funding</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">6</p>
      <p>charts, from Cash Balance to Burn Rate</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">20</p>
      <p>steps in the guided tour of the dashboard</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Step 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Describe the business</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">In one text box, or in a Business Model Canvas.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Step 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Watch the AI build it</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">The builder shows each stage and what the AI understood.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Step 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Explore and adjust</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Change an assumption and the charts below recalculate.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s4">
  <p class="st-k rv">04 · Key UX decisions</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s4">Five decisions that shape the product</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    The tension between what a beginner and an expert need shaped most of these decisions.
  </p>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision A</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Start from the idea, not the spreadsheet</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          The home page opens with one question instead of a form full of financial fields: tell
          SumoSum what you’ll sell, at what price and in what quantity, and the AI builds the
          forecast. There is a microphone for people who would rather talk it through.
        </p>
        <p class="cs__p">
          People who think in a more structured way can switch to <strong>BMC Mode</strong>, a
          Business Model Canvas with its nine blocks, or upload an existing canvas.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="st-figs rv">
      <figure class="cs__fig">
        <img
          src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/sumosum-starter.webp' | rel }}"
          width="1400"
          height="1368"
          alt="SumoSum home page with the headline AI-Powered Forecasting Built on Your Ideas, a prompt box with a microphone, and a tour tooltip marked Step 1 of 7. Below it, a Try Business Model Canvas section shows the nine canvas blocks and a Submit to Get Your Forecast button."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>The AI starter: describe the business in one box, or fill in a canvas</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="cs__fig">
        <img
          src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/sumosum-canvas.webp' | rel }}"
          width="1400"
          height="874"
          alt="Create Project modal titled Let SumoSum AI Build Your Financials, with a two-step progress indicator (Project Details, Prompt), the Business Model Canvas grid and a Single Input toggle."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Inside the app, a new project uses the same two ways in</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision B</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Make the AI understandable</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          A forecast that appears from nowhere is hard to trust. The builder shows each stage with a
          progress bar and a step count, and a <strong>Live Analysis</strong> panel restates the
          business in plain language, so people can spot a misunderstanding before they reach the
          charts.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/sumosum-builder.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1400"
        height="927"
        alt="SumoSum AI forecast builder at Step 9 of 10 and 89 percent progress. On the left, a robot mascot announces Added 1 service: Bookkeeping Service. On the right, a Live Analysis panel summarises the business in a paragraph above a checklist of four stages."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>The forecast builder shows what the AI understood while it works</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision C</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Keep assumptions next to their consequences</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          The dashboard stacks purple <strong>Assumptions</strong> above red
          <strong>Charts &amp; Reports</strong>. Change an input and the charts underneath
          recalculate. Assumptions come in eight categories, each field has an info tooltip, and
          Footnotes and Comments support anyone reviewing the model.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/sumosum-charts.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1400"
        height="874"
        alt="Project dashboard for Buns Hawaii - Sandwich Truck, Base Case. A collapsed purple Assumptions bar sits above a red Charts and Reports panel with six charts: Cash Balance, Income Breakdown, Income vs Expenses, Cash Flow, Use of Funds and Burn Rate. A banner reads Save your Project - FREE! No credit card required."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>The dashboard: assumptions above, the charts they drive below</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision D</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Explain the charts, not just draw them</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          A 20-step guided tour explains each chart, assumption category and tab in plain terms. Burn
          Rate, for example, “shows how quickly you’re using up your available funds”. The tour can
          be skipped at any step, and every chart keeps an info icon for later.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision E</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Let people try before they commit</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          Anyone can build a forecast as a guest. The project header invites them to save it, “FREE!
          No credit card required”, and the tour ends with a prompt to sign up. Value comes first and
          the account comes second.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/sumosum-assumptions.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1400"
        height="878"
        alt="Dashboard with the Assumptions panel open on its category tabs and fields such as Bank Account Interest, Credit Terms, Opening Cash Balance and Income Tax. A tour tooltip marked Step 8 of 20 reads Welcome to your forecasting workspace! with Skip and Next buttons."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>The guided tour introduces the assumptions workspace, one step at a time</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s5">
  <p class="st-k rv">05 · Core screens</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s5">Projects, plans and the admin side</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Around the forecast sit the screens for organising work and managing access. Choose a screen to
    see it.
  </p>
  <div class="st-wide rv">
    <div class="st-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Core screens">
      <button class="st-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="t-pro" aria-controls="p-pro">Projects and scenarios</button>
      <button class="st-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="t-bil" aria-controls="p-bil">Billing</button>
      <button class="st-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="t-adm" aria-controls="p-adm">Admin panel</button>
    </div>
    <div class="st-gal" role="tabpanel" id="p-pro" aria-labelledby="t-pro">
      <p class="st-quote">A business model is a working document</p>
      <p class="cs__p">
        Each project holds one or more scenarios, such as a base case and alternatives, so people can
        try a different price or cost structure without overwriting their first plan. The list shows
        each person’s role and the number of members, which supports the shared way accountants and
        advisors work with business owners.
      </p>
      <figure class="cs__fig">
        <img
          src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/sumosum-projects.webp' | rel }}"
          width="1400"
          height="874"
          alt="Project list with a Project Overview card showing the current plan, billing frequency and total projects available. Below it, projects expand to show their scenarios, with role, member count and Actions on each row."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Projects expand into scenarios, with the current plan and project allowance on top</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="st-gal" role="tabpanel" id="p-bil" aria-labelledby="t-bil">
      <p class="st-quote">What you have, what it costs, how to change it</p>
      <p class="cs__p">
        Plans are based on the number of projects. Billing Information shows the current plan,
        renewal date and extra project slots beside a summary that adds them up, and changing plans
        compares the project count now and after, with a clear warning before anything would be
        lost.
      </p>
      <figure class="cs__fig">
        <img
          src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/sumosum-billing.webp' | rel }}"
          width="1400"
          height="874"
          alt="Billing Information page with Payment and Billing tabs selected. A Current Plan card lists plan, frequency, project limit, purchase and renewal dates and extra projects. A Billing Summary card itemises the plan and additional project slots and shows the next billing total. A Change Plan button sits below."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Billing: the current plan beside a summary of what it costs</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="st-gal" role="tabpanel" id="p-adm" aria-labelledby="t-adm">
      <p class="st-quote">Plans and add-ons in one place</p>
      <p class="cs__p">
        The team manages plans in one table with price, project allowance, subscriber count, status
        and an on/off toggle, and creates and edits plans and add-ons, such as extra projects, from
        the same panel.
      </p>
      <figure class="cs__fig">
        <img
          src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/sumosum-admin.webp' | rel }}"
          width="1400"
          height="874"
          alt="Admin panel Plan List with columns for plan name, price per month, projects, subscribers, status badges, an edit action and a mode toggle. An Add-On card for Extra Project sits below, with an Edit button."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>The admin panel. Figures are placeholder data.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">Beyond the core flow</h3>
  <ol class="st-list st-wide rv">
    <li>Loading screens and a carousel previewing features</li>
    <li>Alert screens</li>
    <li>Mobile and tablet layouts</li>
    <li>The marketing site</li>
    <li>A WordPress plugin of the single-input starter</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s6">
  <p class="st-k rv">06 · Reflection</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s6">More than arranging charts</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Designing for business strategy and financial modelling means understanding how people think
    about decisions, and showing complex relationships without oversimplifying them.
  </p>
  <div class="st-callout rv">
    <p class="st-bh">
      A good strategy tool shouldn’t just produce an answer. It should help people understand the
      assumptions behind it and explore what happens when they change.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
